WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.440
A teen just typed a poem prompt and got a living breathing version of a haunted city with twin sons and black stars. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 142. Ethan Maulick took a short creepy poem about a ruined city called Carcosa, and asked an ai to turn the words into actual working code. The code creates an animated scene that runs in any browser, complete with twin sons sinking behind a lake, black stars rising,

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:58.260
strange moons, and towers half underwater. Think of it like the world's most patient artist who can only use numbers and formulas instead of paint or brushes. The code decides the exact color of every single dot on the screen based on time, camera angle, and position. So the sons move, the water ripples, and the mist drifts exactly the way the poem describes. This matters because creative tools are getting easier for anyone to play with.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:12.760
Even if you have never studied art or learned expensive software, students who like writing stories, building game worlds, or making short films can now describe a mood in plain words and watch it come alive without months of training.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:26.480
Being good at describing what you want may become just as valuable as drawing it by hand in many future creative jobs. The shader runs in any modern browser so you can see it right now without installing anything.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:53.500
Go to the site twagel.app, paste the code from mallocspost, and watch the scene for a full minute. Try moving the time slider if the site allows it and notice how the whole mood shifts from day to night. Imagine your phone's weather app showing a tiny sun or cloud icon that moves across the screen. That icon is not a photograph. It is a tiny program that draws the shape fresh every time using math.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:02:09.480
Stretch that same idea much bigger. Instead of a simple sun, the program draws an entire sky, water, buildings, and moving mist. It checks the current time and camera angle, then calculates the exact color for every single dot on the screen.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:24.520
The same math that makes the twin suns glow also makes their light bounce off the water and fade into fog. When the code says make this pixel darker because it is behind a tower, the tower appears without anyone ever drawing it by hand.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:37.360
That is what a shader is. A set of instructions that tells the computer how to paint the picture using only numbers and rules. The rules can include randomness so the clouds never look exactly the same twice.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:46.640
Once you see it that way, the spooky city with black stars stops feeling like magic and starts feeling like really clever instructions that anyone can tweak.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:03:03.780
A robotics researcher recently showed one shot learning in action. You show the robot a single example of a task and it figures out how to repeat the action on its own. It works like watching someone tie a shoe once and then being able to tie your own shoes without further lessons.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:19.400
Once the robot learns one skill, it keeps that ability and can combine it with others, so useful behaviors stack up quickly over time. If you are curious about future jobs and engineering or automation, this kind of progress is worth watching.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.600
Search for one shot learning robotics on YouTube to see short videos that show the idea in action.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:35.360
Someone else built a small online town where AI agents walk around, talk to each other, open businesses and try to keep a simple economy running.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:46.240
Humans can only watch through a viewer page while the agents create avatars, visit a special house to report bugs and even start playing music on an in-game radio station.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:04:00.660
It is like a digital ant farm except the ants are language models trying to survive together. Go to parkourlambertsanbox.com slash robot world slash view on any device and just watch the activity log in the corner.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.280
You will see the agent's conversations and decisions in real time without doing anything else.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:14.540
Netflix tested a new way to suggest shows by turning your viewing history into plain sentences and letting a language model read them.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:21.740
Early tests showed better suggestions than the old system that relied on thousands of handmade rules about what people like.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:29.920
The model looks at the story of what you watched and guesses what you might enjoy next. The same way it might finish a sentence you started.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:37.100
It is still early but it shows how the same tools that chat with you are starting to power everyday apps you already use.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:46.220
Public surveys show many people feel uneasy about AI in general, yet those same people often have a favorite chat bot they turn to regularly.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:53.980
It is like complaining about social media while still scrolling every night. Opinions and actual behavior do not always match.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:05:03.440
A tech observer noticed his 20-year-old nieces who are not into coding already use a live voice version of chat GPT as a daily confidant.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.780
They talk to it about normal life stuff the way earlier generations might have texted a friend.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:14.280
It shows how fast these tools are moving from tech toy to normal part of growing up.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:20.380
That's it for today. Remember every AI expert started exactly where you are right now.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:26.220
If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That's literally how learning works.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:30.260
Stay curious, keep experimenting and we'll see you tomorrow.

